@meta
id: 27-08
title: Where that setting should have lived
chapter: 27
type: lesson
skills: set-local, filetype
par: 76
minutes: 6
vscode: settings.json, a "[sql]" block with an editor.tabSize inside it
open: after/ftplugin/sql.vim

@brief
Typing `:setlocal shiftwidth=4` every time you open a `.sql` file is not a
workflow. Vim already has a place for "this is how I want SQL files to
behave", and it will read it for you every time one is opened.

When vim decides a buffer's filetype is `sql`, it sources every
`ftplugin/sql.vim` it can find on the runtime path, and then every
`after/ftplugin/sql.vim`. The `after/` ones run last, which is why yours
go there: your settings land on top of whatever the shipped plugin decided,
rather than underneath it.

The file does not exist yet. Vim has opened an empty buffer at that path.
Write the three lines that give SQL buffers four-space indentation with
spaces rather than tabs:

    setlocal shiftwidth=4
    setlocal tabstop=4
    setlocal expandtab

Every line is `setlocal`, and the reason is the whole lesson.

@keys
| `:setlocal opt=…` | the only correct form inside an ftplugin |
| `:set ft?` | what vim thinks this buffer is |
| `:e ~/.vim/after/ftplugin/sql.vim` | where this file lives on a real machine |
| `:scriptnames` | every file vim has sourced this session |

@hints
Nothing clever here — it is a new, empty buffer and three lines of text.
The thinking is in why each one says `setlocal`.
--
`i`, the three lines with Enter between them, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    isetlocal shiftwidth=4<Enter>setlocal tabstop=4<Enter>setlocal expandtab<Esc>:wq<Enter>

Sixty-five keystrokes, sixty of them the text.

Why `setlocal` and never `set`: this file is sourced *every time a SQL
buffer is created*. With `set shiftwidth=4` in it, opening one SQL file
would change the global `shiftwidth`, and every TypeScript file you opened
afterwards would inherit 4. One `.sql` file early in the morning would
quietly reformat your afternoon. `setlocal` touches the buffer that
triggered the load and nothing else.

The layout on a real machine:

    ~/.vim/ftplugin/sql.vim         runs instead of a shipped one
    ~/.vim/after/ftplugin/sql.vim   runs after the shipped one

Use `after/`. The shipped ftplugins are good and you rarely want to
replace one wholesale; you want to disagree with it about two settings.

This is also the answer to a question that comes up in every vim config:
"where do I put a setting that only applies to one language?" Not in an
`if` in your `.vimrc`, and not in an autocommand you wrote by hand. In an
`after/ftplugin` file named after the filetype. Vim is already doing the
detection and the dispatch; join in rather than rebuilding it.

@expected after/ftplugin/sql.vim
setlocal shiftwidth=4
setlocal tabstop=4
setlocal expandtab
